---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { MODES } from "../scripts/modes";

const churchModes = MODES.filter((mode) => mode.group === "church");
const otherScales = MODES.filter((mode) => mode.group === "other");
const pentatonicScales = MODES.filter((mode) => mode.group === "pentatonic");
---

<Layout title="Same melody, different mode — why a scale's shape changes its mood">
  <header>
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="./">Home</a>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main>
    <h1>The same tune, thirteen different moods</h1>
    <p data-testid="intro">
      Every mode below plays the same two-part idea: first a run through all
      of that mode's own notes, then a short, memorable tune shaped the same
      way regardless of how many notes the mode has. What changes is which
      notes each mode picks out, and that alone is enough to make it sound
      bright, sad, bittersweet, exotic or tense. Click a mode to hear it, and
      watch the ring light up.
    </p>

    <details class="mode-primer">
      <summary>What is a mode in music?</summary>
      <p>
        A <strong>mode</strong> (or scale) is a specific pattern of whole and
        half steps chosen from the 12 pitches Western music recognizes.
        Below is all 12, with no mode applied — nothing colored, nothing
        picked out. Every mode you can play on this page, and every scale in
        music generally, is simply a particular subset of these same 12
        notes, arranged in a particular order.
      </p>
      <div id="baseline-wheel" class="wheel wheel-baseline" data-testid="baseline-wheel"></div>
      <p class="hint">
        This is the complete set — every mode below just selects from within
        it. Five- and six-note scales (like the pentatonic scales further
        down) select fewer notes than the seven-note church modes, which is
        why they sound so different even though they draw from the same 12.
      </p>
    </details>

    <section aria-labelledby="wheel-heading">
      <h2 id="wheel-heading">Play a mode</h2>

      <div class="mode-groups">
        <div class="mode-group">
          <h3>Church modes</h3>
          <div class="mode-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Church modes">
            {
              churchModes.map((mode, index) => (
                <button
                  type="button"
                  data-mode-button={mode.id}
                  aria-pressed={index === 0}
                  style={`--mode-color: ${mode.color}`}
                >
                  {mode.name}
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mode-group">
          <h3>Other scales</h3>
          <div class="mode-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Other scales">
            {
              otherScales.map((mode) => (
                <button
                  type="button"
                  data-mode-button={mode.id}
                  aria-pressed={false}
                  style={`--mode-color: ${mode.color}`}
                >
                  {mode.name}
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mode-group">
          <h3>Pentatonic scales</h3>
          <div class="mode-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Pentatonic scales">
            {
              pentatonicScales.map((mode) => (
                <button
                  type="button"
                  data-mode-button={mode.id}
                  aria-pressed={false}
                  style={`--mode-color: ${mode.color}`}
                >
                  {mode.name}
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="mode-label" class="mode-label" data-testid="mode-label"></div>
      <div id="wheel" class="wheel" data-testid="wheel"></div>
      <p class="hint">
        The big circles are the piano's white keys (naturals); the small ones
        nested between them are the black keys (accidentals) — a fixed
        layout, the same for every mode. Whichever notes light up in color
        are this mode's scale, chosen from among those 12; the one with a
        dark outline is the note currently sounding. Each mode plays its own
        full scale first, then the short tune.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
</Layout>
